\f1\fs22 Alexander Koalemos\'97police \'91lieutenant\'92 and feted media celebrity\'97manufactured his famous smile for the enthralled audience. Despite falling ratings, disillusioned sponsors, and increasing pressure to fake evidence, the show must go on. Specifically, the final episode recording for the day must go on. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Good evening my friends. Welcome to another episode of 
\f2\i Candid Criminal
\f1\i0 ,\'92 he said and paused.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91If you hadn\'92t done it, we wouldn\'92t have to vote!\'92 the studio audience chorused back, then erupted in applause and cheers.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, indeed,\'92 Alexander said. \'91Let\'92s not keep out special guest waiting.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Guilty! Guilty! Guilty!\'92 the audience chanted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander chuckled and waved down the noise. \'91Airborne ladies and gentleman, please join me in reviling Cloudborne Hamish Hammersmith.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The audience erupted as two giant body-builder types\'97their skins glistening with oil\'97dragged an emaciated sod before Alexander\'92s raised dias, and dumped him with rehearsed ceremony on the bare studio floorboards.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You have the wrong man,\'92 the sod said, as instructed by his production handler. \'91I haven\'92t done anything.\'92 The audience laughed at his pathetic petition, as Alexander hoped everyone streaming at home would laugh.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Play the package,\'92 Alexander said to camera, exactly as he had hundreds of times before. He hated the money the production team spent on the multiple holographic sequences of \'91evidence\'92 they played. But their design schtick was part of the brand: grainy, high-contrast imaging with most of the colour leached out, all calculated to cast the \'91special guest\'92 as loathsome and reprehensible. Each ended with a brand-conscious dissolve of a Hammersmith\'92s magnified, evil face.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Guilty! Guilty! Guilty!\'92 the audience resumed. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In the early days of the show, Alexander begrudgingly conceded the evidence packages were a major attractant of eyeballs, eclipsed only by his talent as celebrity anchor. Years on, he resented the production costs all the more.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The 
\fs18 QUIET PLEASE
\fs22  sign above the studio floor illuminated. The audience settled to murmurs.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What have you to say now, Hamish Hammersmith?\'92 Alexander read from the autocue. The next part of the show\'97where he challenged the special guest, and tricked them into inconsistent testimony\'97passed with Alexander on autopilot. He\'92d given so many such performances, they were all a blur to him. While they were passing, it wasn\'92t uncommon for him to be reminiscing, say on a meal he\'92d once had, perhaps a spectacular mac and cheese.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s time for the verdict!\'92 Alexander boomed, his engagement with proceedings reignited.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91If you hadn\'92t done it, we wouldn\'92t have to vote!\'92 The audience knew the formula.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Votes poured in. After time had expired, Alexander announced, \'91The votes are in, my friends.\'92 The audience hushed. \'91We received 2.17 million votes. There might be some folks voting more than once, but here on 
\f2\i Candid Criminal
\f1\i0 \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91\'85we value the enthusiasm!\'92 the audience answered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, yes we do!\'92 Alexander sparkled at the camera. No one would deny he was a handsome son of a \'85 military conglomerate magnate mother (with all due fear and respect), but Eloise is for later in the story. Alexander gazed towards the back of the studio as if reading a faraway autocue. He beamed a smile, as if surprised by news. \'91The decision\'92s in, and it\'92s\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Unanimous!\'92 The audience drowned Alexander out, as they always did.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91There you have it, folks! Stay tuned for the sentencing. We\'92ll be right back after these important messages.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Cut!\'92 the director called. Alexander went straight to make-up while an expectant carnival buzz pulsed around the studio.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When recording resumed, Alexander parroted, \'91Thanks for those important messages from our sponsors.\'92 Turn to Camera 3. \'91Remember, for all your family planning needs, Pattinson\'92s Mobile Abortions, come to you, and take your worries away. Just call 1800-2229-4663. That\'92s 1800-
\fs20 BABY-GONE
\fs22 .\'92 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Turn to Camera One. Alexander whipped up the audience with, \'91And now it\'92s time for\'85 !\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The Wheel of Death!\'92 they responded. \'91Wheel\'85of\'85Death. Wheel\'85of\'85Death.\'92 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A large, intentionally low-rent chocolate wheel was wheeled out by a pretty extra in an elaborate head-dress and swimsuit.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91How lucky can you get?\'92 Alexander said. \'91Each episode, we put ten randomly generated punishments on the Wheel of Death. Let\'92s see which will be Hamish Hammersmith\'92s fate. Here are the options. Number 1: boiling in oil.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The audience went, 
\f2\i ooh.
\f1\i0  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Number 2: trebuchet slap.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The audience went, 
\f2\i ahhh
\f1\i0 . \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Number 3 is clubbing to death. And we don\'92t mean drugs and late-night dancing, folks.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The audience laughed excessively less than Alexander craved.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Number 4: shark tank. Number 5: one thousand paper cuts. Number 6 is acid injection. Number 7: packet of peanuts\'97the get-out-of-jail-free card, folks!\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The audience booed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91But I\'92m anaphylactic,\'92 Hammersmith complained weakly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The audience cheered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We know,\'92 Alexander said behind his hand to Hammersmith, loud enough so the mics would pick it up. \'91Number 8: snake pit. Number 9: drowning in a cesspool of fetid dairy products.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92m lactose intolerant,\'92 Hammersmith bemoaned. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And finally, number 10: helium chamber. Your last words sound like a talking chipmunk, so choose them wisely.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91But I haven\'92t done a thing.\'92 Hammersmith shook. His copious tears eroded the dirt from his cheeks and gave him an appearance of frothing at the mouth.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, c\'92mon,\'92 Alexander chided Hammersmith. \'91Spin the Wheel.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Spin the wheel! Spin the wheel! Spin the wheel!\'92 the chorus ascended over the stomping of feet. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hammersmith quaking, approached the wheel. \'91Oh, please! Someone help me!\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>By perhaps the only grit of luck the wretched Cloudborne Hamish Hammersmith ever tasted, someone did help him\'97a squad of riot cops infiltrated the studio and escorted Alexander away.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Show\'92s over, folks,\'92 Alexander beamed from the exit.\
