\f1\fs22 The Wheel of Death was rigged to stop at 
\fs18 TREBUCHET SLAP
\fs22 \'97Alexander\'92s favorite episode climax both for its effect on ratings and for the production intricacies it required to pull off. While all execution scenes were, by necessity, one-take wonders\'97there could be no do-overs\'97the trebuchet slap in particular required perfect timing and execution. 
\f2\i Candid Criminal\'92s
\f1\i0  trebuchet was the typical medieval sturdy wooden siege engine, an 
\f2\i A
\f1\i0 -frame set upon a wheeled platform complete with hulking iron brackets, giant round head nails, ropes and pulleys. A thick beam about six metres long pivoted on an axle attached to the 
\f2\i A-
\f1\i0 frame\'92s apex. At one end of the beam hung a large counterweight; at the other, a lengthy sling and pouch where the convict would be strapped in. The counter-weight would be winched up and ratcheted in place. When the executioner (a swimsuit model, as mandated by every focus group ever) released the ratchet, the counter-weight would fall, and over would sling convict, cradle and all. The magic of 
\f2\i Candid Criminal\'92s 
\f1\i0 corrupted trebuchet was, it would not hurl its payload in the air, such as when laying siege to a castle. No, this trebuchet, by perversion of its design, hurled its hapless passenger straight into the ground within fractured reach of its splintery wheels.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Convict pancake, anyone?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Yes, the trebuchet slap was Alexander\'92s favourite. It was difficult to perfect. In the early days, if a convict appeared to survive the engine, they were pardoned. But freeing convicts lodged awkwardly in the audience\'92s craw, and eventually Marketing demanded a survivor be subject to a second spin of the wheel, usually subsequently rigged to 
\fs20 CLUBBING
\fs22 . As much as his fans loved a double event, Alexander preferred a successful first execution. He was a professional.\
